course: dictionary
lesson: 13580
title: Words for a start
level: primary-5
stars: 1
explain: Incipient is a formal word. It means just beginning to happen or to show. You will meet it in books, science and medicine, not usually in chatty speech. Read the words around it to find its meaning.
explain: «Mum noticed the incipient cold: a sniffle and a tiny cough.» A sniffle and a tiny cough show a cold that has only just started, so incipient means just starting. Check it by swapping in starting: «Mum noticed the starting cold» still makes sense.
explain: «The builder found an incipient crack in the wall, before anyone could see it from the floor.» The crack was not yet obvious, so incipient also means early, before something is fully grown or easy to see. In everyday speech, use starting, early or beginning instead.
fact: Incipient is an adjective, and it is a formal word.
fact: «Incipient» means just beginning to happen or to show.
fact: «Incipient» can also mean in an early stage, before something is fully grown or obvious.
fact: Everyday words you can use instead are starting, early and beginning.
fact: Phrases you may meet: incipient signs, an incipient crack, at an incipient stage.
exercise: «The doctor spotted incipient signs of a cold: a little sneeze and a sore throat.» What does incipient mean here? | *Just starting | Hot and sunny | Fully grown and very bad | Gone away for good
exercise: Ben says, «The builder found an incipient crack in the wall.» Which everyday word could replace incipient? | empty | enormous | *early | painted
exercise: Copy the word from this sentence that means just beginning: «The incipient fire was tiny, so Dad put it out with one bucket of water.» | *incipient | bucket | tiny | water
test: «The teacher noticed an incipient problem with the class plants: just one yellow leaf.» What does incipient mean? | Fully dead | *Just beginning to show | Nicely painted | Very large
test: Which phrase describes something at an incipient stage? | A tree that is fully grown and tall | A loud and busy market | *Something at an early stage, not yet obvious | A finished cake on the table
exam: Which sentence uses incipient correctly? | *The incipient signs of spring were tiny green buds on the trees. | The incipient river was wide and flowing very fast. | The incipient dinosaur was fully grown and roaring. | The incipient birthday party had been going on for hours.
